# Section Heading Labels (Eyebrows)

Every marketing **section** H2 must have a `SectionEyebrow` immediately above it: short dash + label (e.g. "Features"), matching `Components/ui/SectionHeading.tsx`.

## Required pattern

```tsx
import {
  SectionEyebrow,
  SectionHeading,
  SectionLead,
} from "@/Components/ui/SectionHeading";

// Centered sections
<SectionEyebrow align="center">Features</SectionEyebrow>
<SectionHeading id="feature-pillars-heading" withEyebrow>
  …
</SectionHeading>

// Left-aligned hubs (products / resources)
<SectionEyebrow>Verification</SectionEyebrow>
<SectionHeading id="…" withEyebrow>…</SectionHeading>

// Dark bands
<SectionEyebrow align="center" tone="onDark">How it works</SectionEyebrow>
```

- Always set `withEyebrow` on `SectionHeading`, or `mt-3` on a raw `<h2>`.
- Labels are short (1-3 words): Features, Pricing, FAQ, How it works, Testimonials, Integrations, Results, Use cases.
- Prefer `SectionEyebrow` over custom pills, uppercase overlines, or SVG badges.
- Do not invent a second eyebrow component.

## Do not add eyebrows to

- Hero / page H1 areas
- CTA sections (`CTA`, `LandingFinalCta`, `BlogCta`, `ToolCta`)
- Header and Footer
- Dialog titles, card titles, API endpoint H2s, legal/guide body H2s, stats strips without a section H2

## Anti-patterns

```tsx
// BAD: heading spacing for eyebrow with no eyebrow rendered
<SectionHeading withEyebrow>…</SectionHeading>

// BAD: custom label instead of SectionEyebrow
<p className="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-primary">Features</p>
<h2>…</h2>

// BAD: eyebrow on CTA / hero
```
